<Layout
  title="GPT-6.2 Astra - A.I. - Rycode"
  description="GPT-6.2 Astra, OpenAI’s frontier model for hard professional work, released 17 November 2026 at $8 input, $0.40 cached input, and $40 output per million tokens, with a 1.05M context window."
  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">OpenAI · GPT-6.2</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">GPT-6.2 Astra</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">GPT-6.2 Astra is OpenAI’s new frontier model “for your hardest projects,” released 17 November 2026 alongside <a href="/ai/models/openai/gpt-6-2-sol/">GPT-6.2 Sol</a>, per <a href="https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-6-2-astra-sol/">Introducing GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol</a> [1]. OpenAI says both models “deliver better results on demanding tasks with fewer output tokens” [1]. The API id, the name a program uses to call the model, is <code>gpt-6.2-astra</code> [1][2]. Context is 1,050,000 tokens, with up to 922,000 input tokens and 128,000 output tokens, per the <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.2-astra">GPT-6.2 Astra API model page</a> [2]. Standard API prices are $8 per million input tokens, $0.40 per million cached input tokens, and $40 per million output tokens. OpenAI says input and output are 20% lower than <a href="/ai/models/openai/gpt-6-astra/">GPT-6 Astra</a>, with a 60% discount on cached input [1][2]. Tokens are the word pieces models are billed by.</p>
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />
    <ModelFacts />
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Training</h2>
    <p>Parameter count, training compute, dataset, and training method are not in the sources on this page. The API model page lists a knowledge cutoff of 18 May 2026 [2].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Inference</h2>
    <p>The API model page lists text and image input and text output, and says the API rejects requests that set reasoning effort to <code>none</code> [2]. OpenAI says Astra “writes cleaner code and builds frontends that look and work better,” and that its slides, spreadsheets, and documents “more closely follow the templates you provide” [1]. Tokens per second and rate limits are not in the sources on this page.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Context</h2>
    <p>Context is how much text the model can take in and write in one job. The API model page lists a 1,050,000-token context window, 922,000 max input tokens, and 128,000 max output tokens [2]. For requests with more than 272K input tokens, input and cache rates double and output costs 1.5 times the short-context rate for the full request [2].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Benchmarking</h2>
    <p>Artificial Analysis, an independent tester, scores GPT-6.2 Astra 62 on its Intelligence Index v5.0.1 at max effort, second only to <a href="/ai/models/anthropic/claude-fable-5-2/">Claude Fable 5.2</a> [3][4]. Artificial Analysis says Astra “jumps 5 points from GPT-6 Astra,” and that it is flat with GPT-6 Astra on its Coding Agent Index [3]. It also reports a 59% hallucination rate at max effort on AA-Omniscience [3]. This score is on this site’s <a href="/ai/#aiBench">benchmark table</a>.</p>
    <p>OpenAI’s own claims, not independently checked here: on Agents’ Last Exam, Astra scores 5.2 points higher than GPT-6 Astra “at less than a third of the cost,” and it sets a new high on Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1, 9 percentage points above GPT-6 Astra [1]. None of these tests match the columns of this site’s benchmark table.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Access and pricing</h2>
    <p>OpenAI says that “starting today,” GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol “are available to all Plus, Pro, Enterprise, and Edu users in ChatGPT Work and Codex,” and that developers can use <code>gpt-6.2-astra</code> through the OpenAI API, with Ultrafast available at launch [1].</p>
